+# Repository Guidelines
+
+## Project Structure & Module Organization
+
+This is a Linux kernel source tree. Core code lives in `kernel/`, memory management in `mm/`, filesystems in `fs/`, networking in `net/`, architecture code in `arch/`, and drivers in `drivers/`. Public headers are under `include/`. Build and maintenance helpers live in `scripts/` and `tools/`; examples are in `samples/`. Tests are mainly in `tools/testing/selftests/` and KUnit code under `lib/kunit/`. Read `Documentation/` for subsystem guidance and `MAINTAINERS` for review ownership.
+
+## Build, Test, and Development Commands
+
+- `make defconfig`: create a baseline `.config` for the current architecture.
+- `make menuconfig`: interactively adjust kernel configuration.
+- `make -j$(nproc)`: build the configured kernel and modules.
+- `make -j$(nproc) M=drivers/foo`: build one external-style subtree or driver directory.
+- `make W=1 path/to/file.o`: compile a focused object with extra warnings.
+- `make headers_check`: validate exported UAPI headers.
+- `make kselftest`: run configured kernel selftests.
+- `./tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py run`: run KUnit tests when Python dependencies are available.
+- `scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict <patch>`: check patch style before submission.
+
+## Coding Style & Naming Conventions
+
+Follow `Documentation/process/coding-style.rst`: tabs for indentation, 8-column tab stops, braces and spacing in kernel style, and short helper functions where practical. Prefer existing subsystem helpers and locking patterns over new abstractions. C identifiers use lowercase with underscores; macros and Kconfig symbols use uppercase names. Keep user-visible ABI changes in `include/uapi/` stable and documented. Run `.clang-format` only on deliberately scoped code, not whole legacy files.
+
+## Testing Guidelines
+
+Build-test the smallest relevant target first, then the touched subsystem or full kernel if interfaces changed. Add selftests in `tools/testing/selftests/<subsystem>/` for user-visible behavior and KUnit tests for isolated kernel logic. Name tests after the behavior they validate, and keep them runnable locally and through `make kselftest`.
+
+## Commit & Pull Request Guidelines
+
+Recent history uses subsystem-prefixed subjects such as `iommu/ummu-core: support TID sharing within same mm` and downstream tags like `[OLK-6.6]` where applicable. Use imperative, concise subjects, include the affected subsystem prefix, and explain the problem, fix, and validation in the body. Sign off commits with `Signed-off-by` when submitting patches. Pull requests should identify the target subsystem, link issues or bug reports, list test commands and results, and note config or platform dependencies.
+
+## Agent-Specific Instructions
+
+Do not overwrite local user changes. Keep edits scoped to the requested subsystem, preserve generated files unless explicitly asked, and prefer `rg`, `git grep`, and targeted builds for investigation.
